I was never showed it was about training  for the sake of training that appealed  to me I've always been a fan of martial  arts rock climbing and parkour but  whereas I've trained those things for  radically I've always stuck to my  strength training and my general fitness  routine lately though I've been  reflecting on this and I've come to some  kind of answer I think that training the  way I do appeal to me because it  develops traits and attributes rather  than skills as such when there isn't  enough time in the day to pursue all of  my interests in activities training  strength speed agility balance and even  focus helps me to improve in all of  those things I call this super  functional training functional training  is training to become more comfortable  and stronger during everyday tasks  sometimes it means training to  supplement your abilities in a  particular sport or athletic event super  functional training then means training  to be able to do more than is required  by everyday life or to train in ways  that will benefit sports that you never  plan to compete in and I think that  everyone should train this way they  shouldn't treat their training just like  rehab to help them feel and move a  little bit better the way they were  supposed to be able to do in the first  place they shouldn't just treat it  mainly just to build a sexy six-pack and  not only for a specific skill or sport I  think we should all train to be the very  best that we can be a trained martial  artist will kick your ass no matter how  strong you are the coordination and  muscle memory required to deliver a  perfect punch is far more important than  the muscle behind it but while that is  true a martial artists will become  better at their craft by building more  strength speed reflexes and mobility  someone who's trained all these  attributes will likewise last a little  bit longer against the trained martial  artist  they look why are the skills necessary  to fight more quickly and if you take  two people with no fight training then  the fitter stronger one will win by  training mobility their kicks and knees  will reach further increasing their  likelihood of landing a hit by training  strength they will have access to  greater reserves of power even the  perfect technique is limited by the size  of the muscles and by training endurance  still lasts longer before their strike  start to lose that power of speed this  is why I think that Conor McGregor's  trading with Egypt portal the movement  guru is far more than just a publicity  stunt actually makes a lot of sense and  it's why Brazilian jiu-jitsu benefits  from kettlebell  Bell training to open up and strengthen  the shoulders and to increase stability  likewise being fast and explosive will  make you better at parkour and sports  greater muscle endurance and grip  strength will help with rock climbing  being focused and having greater  emotional control helps the athlete or  the orator not to croak under pressure a  working memory with more capacity will  allow us put bullets as visualized  positions of every player on the pitch  having a stronger core will make you  better at pretty much everything of  course all these traits can help outside  of athletic pursuits too whether you're  gardening moving furniture or  armwrestling a friend all these things  benefit from being strong fast agile and  focus and they helped me to be ready for  the unknown further things you can't  predict to prepare for that's the reason  I love training it helps to improve  performance across the board so that  you're able to rise to any challenge and  perform in any capacity you don't have  to narrow your options by choosing just  one thing to excel at it drastically  increases the number of options  available to you at any moment but only  if you train the right way and that's  why I like this kind of training in  particular and it's why I believe the  cross modal training is superior to  doggedly focusing on just one aspect of  performance because if all you're doing  is the three big lifts in an attempt to  get stronger that won't translate the  greater performance in parkour or  martial arts it won't help you to run  faster or swim a mile you may be  extremely strong but you're only used to  exerting that strength in one plane of  movement your body composition may be  slowing you down your range of motion  might be missing in some areas and  entire aspects of your strength will  likely be overlooked how is your  straight arm strength your shoulder  mobility your rotational strength  bodybuilding create strength with lower  peak output is compared to powerlifting  but greater endurance thanks to those  longer rep ranges the crafts are super  heroic physique and it targets muscles  missed by powerlifting but it also does  little to Train intramuscular  coordination or maximum muscle fiber  recruitment and it still misses out  whole crucial muscle groups like the  obliques  calisthenics will build your straight  arm strength for sure it will create  amazing relative strength that helped  you to perform incredible gravity  defying movements the handstands trains  and men's four prior section of body  control  but calisthenics alone can provide no  substitute for the heavy squat or  deadlift a plyometric jump squat is just  not the same as loading a huge amount of  weight onto your shoulders and then  bracing against it as you move into a  deep squat for multiple reps  that builds huge peak strength  intramuscular coordination and denser  bones even nor can calisthenics provide  the same kind of hypertrophy and muscle  size as bodybuilding due to the maximum  weight available though one type of  training hits every attribute needed for  complete performance but each brings  something new and important to the table  kettlebells will open up your shoulders  build your work capacity and provide a  ballistic strength not to be confused  with plyometric or explosive strength  high-intensity interval training and  metabolic conditioning will increase  your lactate threshold and your  mitochondrial count low intensity  steady-state cardio meanwhile will build  will and determination while growing  your left ventricle for lower resting  heart rate and superior physical health  yoga Pilates and gymnastic strength  training will increase your free range  of motion and doing the stiffness that  comes from years of sitting and  repeating the same few movement over and  over again the old-time strongman  favorite of overcoming isometrics will  develop crazy grip strength and enhanced  muscle fiber recruitment passive quasi  isometrics creates amazing muscle  control making sure you aren't weak at  any point in the movement and teaching  you to move more efficiently by  contracting only the muscles you truly  need during a given Reitman  don't even be limited to things we  typically think of as training wrote  it's from science from history and from  forums online think outside the box and  realize that everything can be trained  meditation helps to stay focused and on  task mental toughness will ensure that  you keep going and stay strong in your  lowest moments ambidexterity will help  to perform equally well with all four  limits now if you take someone who's  trained it in all these different  disciplines and then get them to throw a  punch for the first time they'll be able  to call upon impressive max strength and  power lifting powerful torque through  the obliques thanks to calisthenics and  a powerful posterior chain from cattle  swings  they'll be focused quick to learn and  able to loosen their entire body before  contracting the moment of impact when  sparring they'll last longer in the ring  when throwing kicks will benefit from  superior balance and mobility for those  same skills will also help them to climb  to flow over obstacles to win games of  chess and to excel at any sport I'm not  saying don't specialize if you want to  compete at the highest levels in  powerlifting or long-distance running go  for it and choosing a skill be it dance  martial arts or memorizing decks of  cards and honing it to perfection is a  noble pursuit indeed but if you're not  training for anything in particular then  train to be the most well-rounded  version of yourself  don't just get obsessed with  on one specific list don't just train  your amps don't just fix your bad  posture don't train to be better at what  you already are trained for what you  wants to become so why not have a go at  climbing that rope you've noticed in the  corner of the gym why not get outside  your comfort zone and try a heavy  deadlift or alternatively a pursued a  planche push-ups why not train your  brain just like you train your body and  whoever you are don't be jealous and  tribal by sticking viciously to your one  kind of training claiming superiority  recognize the unique value that all  these systems have to offer and it's  just the icing on the cake that training  this way also keeps things exciting  action-packed and varied training should  be the furthest thing from boring if you  don't like working out because it's  boring then you're doing something wrong  so thanks Sam for watching this one guys  I hope you found it interesting and  useful let me know what you think in the  comments down below do you combine  multiple different training methods do  you see value in it or would you rather  stick to one thing and be a master of  one rather than the jack-of-all-trades  if you want to learn more about this  kind of training then you can check out  my ebook and training program super  functional training in the link in the  description down below alternatively  just subscribe to this channel I've got  loads more cool stuff on the way thanks  a ton for watching this one guys and  I'll see you next time  bye for now  there will knock us to the ground at the  ground they will knock us to the ground
 

